## Reach

On one occasion, a woman I knew spent five minutes directing my attention, through an avalanche of words, intensely toward my right hand. At the end of it I felt every bone, each joint, I felt the blood running through my veins and skin wrapping around the flesh. The right hand felt very different from the left. Attention is powerful.

In the times when we used to roam the savanna, attention helped us to find ripe fruit and evade venomous snakes. It makes it possible for us to learn, create, connect. It amplifies our senses so we can really see, feel, smell. And equally important: being seen and felt (and smelled) by others.

In Latin, *attendere* means to stretch. To reach. But what are we reaching towards in a feed? "Instagram brings you closer to the people and things you love", states the tagline on the [about pages](https://about.instagram.com/). It promises a nourishing meal but it is serving us fast food (<i>feed</i> - get it?), a beautiful image of a hamburger held together by pins and glue all pretty, but inedible. Much of the feed is an empty shell of real experiences: feet in the grass, laugh with a friend, playing an instrument, walk in the forest, the smell of food being prepared.

These are experiences that make us content and happy. But the Instagram experience is not optimized for contentment or happiness. It is quite simple: you can not sell anti-ageing cream to a person who does not care about wrinkles. If you are happy with what you have, why would you need more? By participating in algorithmic feeds, we are reaching towards systems designed to make us anxious about our bodies, career, politics, our very existence. And conventiently offering solutions. But do we really need any of them?

I don't think I do.

<figure class="graphic">
  <img
    src="./media/cash-cow-growth.png"
