    alt="Two isometric blocky cows compared: a tiny pink cow representing Instagram's 2015 revenue of 0.5 billion dollars, dwarfed by a much larger one for 2025's 83.6 billion — the cow grew a lot."
  />
  <figcaption>
    <span class="src">Source: <a href="https://resourcera.com/data/social/instagram-revenue/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resourcera</a> (estimates)</span>
  </figcaption>
</figure>

The feed does not only capture, but also degrades our attention. While stretching is a slow, deliberate movement, scrolling reduces our attention to a twitch. Just a quick reflex, a spasm designed to hijack our brains reward mechanism. It forces us to trade intention for impulse. As a result, any such engineered information stream can unconsciously [steer us toward goals that are not our own](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/3F8D7BA2C0FE3A7126A4D9B73A89415D/9781108429092AR.pdf/Stand_out_of_our_Light.pdf?event-type=FTLA). In the short term, this distracts us from what we actually want to do. Sew a dress. Play with our kid. Go for a run. In the long term, it might chip away on our ability to pursue and achieve goals that really matter to us. Make our own clothes. Be a good parent. Be fit.

While this is an environment that we are forced into now, mostly by peer pressure, it does not have to be this way. We can make spaces where the algorithm aims to make us calmer. More educated. More empathetic. More cooperative. Or where it does nothing at all.

<SubscribeInline />

## Release

For me, richness in life comes from movement. Being up and about with wind in my hair, dancing, fingers in soil, or shaping clay. Senses touching the environment, smoke from a fire, warmth on my skin, blackbird's song on the air.

Getting sucked into a feed I stop breathing, stop feeling thirst. I stop feeling like a body in a world. Moreover, I spend time in an environment where the engineers don't give a damn about my well being. Where I am being actively manipulated. Made by a company that has [repeatedly been sued](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawsuits_involving_Meta_Platforms) for fueling addiction and knowingly harming their users mental health.

So I left. My thumb does not look for the icon anymore. There is less to do on my phone. And I read books. And learn to play piano. I call my mom. And I am finding out that life is far too rich to even miss the feed.

In an old anecdote, Alexander the Great walked up to a sun bathing Diogenes, the Greek cynic philosopher that lived in a barrel, and asked him what he desired. Diogenes simply answered: "Stand out of my sun."

As surely as a guy in a barrel could diss the king, we can refuse to have our attention milked for Zuckerbergs private island. Ditch Meta's cash cow. Get some sun.

