  </figcaption>
</figure>

Leaving something behind creates space to think about what we are really searching for, and find alternatives.

When an overwhelming urge to share comes over me now, I send out a couple of private messages. Have a little conversation. Check on how people are doing. I also discovered that some people actually look at the stories sections on WhatsApp and Signal. So I sometimes share there. It's cute and so it doesn't keep me scrolling.

Looking for inspiration, I might go for actual printed magazines. Like [MacGuffin](https://www.macguffinmagazine.com/) or [Fukt](https://www.fuktmagazine.com/). I go through them regularly and explore, engaging with the content, reading essays and  interviews with artists, look repeatedly at the same things and discover something new every time. It's so different from just swiping away to the next and next and next.

I love making photos just for the pretty pictures sake, so I became a [Wikimedia](https://commons.wikimedia.org) contributor. Now [my photos](https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=Special:ListFiles/Heyimada&ilshowall=1) are out there and free of copyright, so anybody can use them if they like. The spirit of the Wikipedia project, passion for information and free sharing of knowledge, resonates way more with my own values, and so it feels good to contribute.

There are more projects out there, like the [Fediverse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fediverse), where [Mastodon](https://joinmastodon.org/) is the Twitter alternative and [pixelfed](https://pixelfed.org/) is the Instagram alternative. These projects copy the format of popular social media, but put them into the hands of the people.

We don't have to reach for the spaces that just suck us in. We can choose where to put our attention. We can make spaces of our own. So get out there, my dear reader. Reach for the spaces that are good for you, and fill them with beautiful things.

